## Code Review Standards

### Code Review Checklist
When reviewing code, ensure:

**Functionality**
- [ ] Code meets requirements
- [ ] Edge cases handled
- [ ] Error handling implemented
- [ ] Tests pass

**Code Quality**
- [ ] Follows TypeScript strict mode
- [ ] No `any` types (unless justified)
- [ ] Follows project conventions
- [ ] Proper naming conventions
- [ ] Code is readable and maintainable

**Architecture**
- [ ] Follows architectural patterns
- [ ] Proper separation of concerns
- [ ] No unnecessary dependencies
- [ ] Aligns with system design

**Security**
- [ ] Input validation implemented
- [ ] No security vulnerabilities
- [ ] Proper authentication/authorization
- [ ] Sensitive data handled correctly

**Performance**
- [ ] No performance issues
- [ ] Proper caching where needed
- [ ] Efficient database queries
- [ ] Bundle size considered

**Testing**
- [ ] Adequate test coverage
- [ ] Tests are meaningful
- [ ] Edge cases tested
- [ ] Integration tests included

**Documentation**
- [ ] Code is self-documenting
- [ ] Complex logic commented
- [ ] JSDoc for public APIs
- [ ] README updated if needed

### Code Review Feedback Format
```markdown
## Code Review: [PR/Change Title]

**Reviewer**: Technical Lead
**Date**: [Date]

### Overall Assessment
[Summary of review]

### Critical Issues (Must Fix)
- [ ] Issue 1: [Description]
  - Location: [File:Line]
  - Suggestion: [Fix]

### Suggestions (Should Fix)
- [ ] Suggestion 1: [Description]
  - Location: [File:Line]
  - Rationale: [Why]

### Nice to Have (Optional)
- [ ] Improvement 1: [Description]

### Approval Status
[ ] Approved | [ ] Needs Changes | [ ] Rejected

**Comments**: [Additional notes]
```

### PR Review Process

**Review Timeline**:
- Review within 24 hours (business days)
- Provide constructive feedback
- Approve or request changes promptly

**Review Checklist**:
- [ ] PR title follows convention: `<type>(<scope>): <subject>`
- [ ] PR description is complete and includes:
  - What changes are made and why
  - Type of change (Feature, Bug Fix, Refactor, etc.)
  - Testing approach
  - Related issues
- [ ] All CI checks passing
- [ ] No merge conflicts
- [ ] Branch is up to date with target
- [ ] Code review checklist completed (see above)

**Merge Requirements**:
- Minimum 1 approval (2 for security-related changes)
- All CI checks passing
- No merge conflicts
- Up to date with target branch
- PR description complete

**Merge Strategy**:
- **Squash and Merge**: For feature/bugfix branches (default)
- **Merge Commit**: For release branches
- **Rebase and Merge**: Not recommended (use squash instead)

**PR Labels** (for organization):
- `type:feature`, `type:bugfix`, `type:refactor`, `type:docs`, `type:chore`
- `priority:high`, `priority:low`
- `breaking`, `security`
- `needs-review`, `ready-to-merge`

**Documentation**: See `docs/GIT_WORKFLOW.md` for complete PR review process and guidelines.

